@page "/spreadsheet-custom-cell-types"

<RadzenText TextStyle="TextStyle.H2" TagName="TagName.H1" class="rz-pt-8">
    Spreadsheet <strong>Custom Cell Types</strong>
</RadzenText>
<RadzenText TextStyle="TextStyle.Subtitle1" TagName="TagName.P" class="rz-pb-4">
    Create custom cell renderers and editors to display progress bars, star ratings, and other visual elements in spreadsheet cells.
</RadzenText>

<RadzenExample ComponentName="Spreadsheet" Example="SpreadsheetCustomCellTypes"
    AdditionalSourceCodePages=@(new [] { "ProgressCellRenderer.razor", "RatingCellRenderer.razor", "RatingCellEditor.razor", "StatusCellEditor.razor" })>
    <SpreadsheetCustomCellTypes />
</RadzenExample>

<RadzenText Anchor="spreadsheet-custom-cell-types#custom-editors" TextStyle="TextStyle.H5" TagName="TagName.H2" class="rz-pt-12">
    Custom Editors
</RadzenText>
<RadzenText TextStyle="TextStyle.Body1" class="rz-mb-4">
    A custom editor receives a <code>SpreadsheetCellEditContext</code> parameter named <code>Context</code>. Text editors such as the Status editor
    focus their input when they open, start from <code>Context.Value</code> and update it as the user types, so the text is committed when the user
    moves to another cell. Characters typed to start editing are passed on to the input. Call <code>Context.CommitAsync</code> to commit, for example
    on Enter, as the Status editor does, or when a value is picked, as the Rating editor does. Escape cancels the edit, and other keys, as well as copy
    and paste, stay inside the editor.
</RadzenText>
